weiner@TheWorld.com (Sam Weiner) writes:

> In article <3D6D566F.7E975AE1@Colorado.edu>,
> Brent Rice  <Randall.Rice@Colorado.edu> wrote:
> >I just had the boot drive on one of my Tru64 boxes crash, so I am
> >wanting to throw my other hard drives that were in that computer into
> >another Tru64 box.  Portions of these drives were set up in advfs
> >domains.  I now want to mount these advfs potions onto the other
> >machine.  Right now, the new machine sees the advfs portion of the
> >drive, but I do not know how to mount and access it.
> >
> >Any direction would be greatly appreciated!
> 
> [smw] I've never used it but advscan would seem to be the
> first step.  man -k advfs is your friend.

Suggest you need to mount your_domain and data_fileset from 
partition d on disk2:

cd /etc/fdmns; mkdir your_domain && cd your_domain; 
ln -s /dev/disk/dsk2d; 

Test by issuing:
showfsets -k your_domain

Mount by:
mkdir -p /mount/point
mount -v -t advfs your_domain#data_fileset /mount/point

Edit the /etc/fstab with one line:
your_domain#data_fileset /mount/point      advfs rw 0 2

Additionally
disklabel /dev/rdisk/dsk2c 
-would be convinient to check your disks type, model and partition
table before you start mounting the wanted partiton.
If you have more disks (volumes) in the domain I've just
linked all of them in the same manner.

In TU64 v4.x the device you link is /dev/rz2d (where "2" is scsiID2)

Regards,
Kjell Andresen  Systems administrator, University of Oslo, Norway
                Center for Information Technology Services and
                Department of Geophysics



