Just pulling your chain a bit....

Which 'America' would that be...North America, Central America, or South
America?

A citizen of 'America' could be any resident from any of those regions.

More correctly, (in a fully qualified form) you are a citizen of the United
States of America, which is merely one of many countries in 'America', and
one of several countries in North America. Geopolitically speaking,
Canadians and Mexicans have as much claim on the term 'American' as anyone
in the United States of America.

Much as a 'European' is a resident of any one of a number of countries
located in Europe (I just know I'm going to get some mail over this one
too).

As to the part of your statement "the greatest country in the world, by the
way" -  don't let your hubris carry you away.....this is an ephemeral state
of affairs as history has shown time and again...... Egypt, Greece, Rome,
China, Macedonia (remember Alexander the Great?), Ottoman Empire, Spain,
England, the United States of America, each a 'superpower' in their own
right, with their own contributions to the advancement of world, each with
their own form of terror/enslavement/dominance, and their own form of
'Manifest Destiny'.

Who's next? Perhaps China yet again in the next 20-50 years. Look at past
history - most of the above noted countries were totalitarian or autocratic
regimes. Arguably Greece, England and the Untied States are/were
quasi-democratic at the time, perhaps Greece more so than England or the
Untied States in pure political science terms. But think about what made
these countries great...running an economic surplus and producing much of
what the world wanted. Today the United States is the world's largest debtor
and China and southeast Asia is making much of what the world wants.
England's decline wasn't so much the result of the rise of the United
States, but more an accident of geography in 1914.

 Remember the immortal words of Casey Stengle - "It's like deja vu all over
again."


