"Kenneth Farmer" <kfarmer@openvms.org> wrote in message
news:X_Wb9.57802$Xa.3094329@twister.southeast.rr.com...

...

> Try to do or say something positive and you become a lackey, is that what
> you think JF?

I can't answer for JF, but it's not what I think.  However, I *do* think
that such efforts can be severely misguided if not *very* carefully
directed.

> Do you hate HP more than you love OpenVMS?

The problem is that this is exactly the trade-off that Compaq created.  And
I suspect it did so knowingly.  "Hey, the only VMS customers left are those
who either can't leave VMS or won't leave VMS.  So what if we kick 'em in
the nuts:  what are they going to do about it?  Hell, they'll be so
uncomfortable with the idea that their vendor is so sleazy that they might
have to look elsewhere that they'll stitch their eyes shut before admitting
it.  And the few that might admit it will just leave quietly."

  Do you want to
> help promote it or do you want to continue to drag it through the mud by
> striking out at the merger and HP management?

You are confused.  Criticism of the merger and HP management in no way drags
VMS through the mud, just its owners - and justifiably so.

  This continued bashing is not
> getting anyone anywhere.

Not true according to Terry, who has stated more than once that it's giving
significant help to cHumPaq's competitors.  While I would like to believe
this, I don't trust Terry any farther than I could drop-kick him (which
likely wouldn't be very far, but I'd sure try for all the distance I could
get given the opportunity):  I suspect it's just another ploy to try to get
people to shut up and just accept that they've been screwed and can't do
diddly-squat about it.

Which, of course, is entirely incorrect:  they *are* doing something about
it, enough to make at least some people nervous about its effect.  And this
is a prime climate in which to continue:  confidence in the integrity of
corporations is at a major low (with good reason, some of which we've seen
right here), and the economy has HP in a tighter place than it's likely
comfortable with.

>
> I understand that you're upset because Alpha was terminated and you think
> OpenVMS deserves more attention.  It's not too late for OpenVMS.

That's debatable.  But what's not at all in question is that the current HP
leadership cannot be trusted:  they'd swear on a stack of Bibles to love,
honor, and cherish VMS for eternity and then drop it in a second if they
felt like it.

So that leaves a couple of possibilities:  either push HP hard enough to get
Carly&Curly to make some *real* (read:  financial) commitments to VMS, with
immediately-funded promotion and new development (sincerity would also be
nice, but is not an available option with those two), or push HP hard enough
to get rid of C&C and get some untainted management in that at least *might*
be amenable to reasoning with.

And neither possibility is as far-fetched as it might appear.  Do you really
believe that Curly would still be Compaq's CEO now if the merger hadn't
temporarily saved his ass?  Or that Carly would still be head of HP (though
that's less certain:  she could talk a fox out of a chicken coop)?  They
bought an extra year or so by confusing the issue with the merger, but
there'll still be an accounting and the ledger isn't looking all that good
right now - so even a small amount of strategically-applied force could have
a disproportionate effect.

 Stick and
> drive man!  We need to pick up the ball and run with it.  The glass is
half
> full.

And you are touchingly naive.  Which is not a mortal sin, but neither a good
rallying point.

  The merger might not have gone the way you/we preferred but that's ok
> because we refuse to lay down, get on your feet and fight for OpenVMS.

The way to fight for VMS is to fight for better management of it.  Without
that, any external efforts will very likely be completely wasted ("Don't
worry, Mom!  We'll just go down to the scrapyard, get some parts, and build
a tractor so we can save the farm!" works only in children's stories, and
not very good ones at that).  But *with* proper management, any such
external efforts will be magnified.

Your way has been tried - exhaustively, and with many variations - for most
of the last decade.  It hasn't worked, and there's no reason whatsoever to
think that it will work now.  But while the carrot has failed, now happens
to be a very good time indeed to try the stick.

- bill





