Hi,

I just upgraded from dual TU-58 tape drives for a VT-103 to a 3.5" dual
floppy set-up from a company called Greco Systems.

The reasons for the conversion are obvious.  However the primary reason was
due to unreliable performance from the TU-58's, or so it seemed.  I am
running RT-11 V04.00E and the system is used to run an antiquated sputtering
machine/tool (semi-conductor) called a Balzers LLS801.

Before the conversion:  To run the system, the LLS801 program, "LLS801.SAV",
is loaded from the System tape (DD0:) and the programs (recipes) are loaded
from the Program tape (DD1:)  The terminal usually always boots up fine and
the LLS801 program loads except  for the MON-F-Swap error message that
appears right after the time and date are entered (this was eliminated later
by manually copying system files and making the disk bootable).    However,
a necessary step in running this machine is having the ability to select a
different program or recipe through LLS801 program from a directory
maintained on DD1:.

The Problem:  When a new recipe was selected from DD0: to be loaded, the
LLS801 program halts and the following error message appears:

    ?Err 23 Hardware I/O error  025602
    in routine "RPARA " line?
    from routine "TTSWIT" line?
    from routine "V4TMNG" line?
    ?MON-F-Swap error

An Aside:  There was and still is nobody well versed with the RT-11 O/S nor
any knowledge of the PDP-11.  I assumed the task of finishing this
conversion because most everyone else has lost patience, and virtually no
one has any desire to become knowledgable of this aged system and/or
software.  I, on the other hand have become fascinated with it and the
following that still exists and have started reading everything I can get my
hands on regarding the afore mentioned.

After the conversion:  Getting the files moved over to 3.5" floppy proved to
be the biggest hurdle followed closely by the lame technical support and
documentation provided by Greco Systems.  I managed to get the System and
the Program(recipe) tapes copied to floppies.  After approximately 2 months
of working on this, we still have the above error.  Thinking it might be the
image copied over from the Program (recipe) floppy, I re-formatted and
reinitialized the floppy only to find that when I would try to save a recipe
I just wrote using the LLS801.SAV program, the same error appeared.  The
only way to successfully save the recipe, would be to exit the recipe
modification screen and enter the Run display screen which seemed to load
everything into memory.  Once I did this, I could exit to the main menu,
then select the save option.  This would save the recipe without generating
the error.  The error would  also occur when a new recipe was selected.  The
LED for DD1: would illuminate briefly, then the error would display.  I also
re-initialized the system floppy (even with a third party program called
Windows RT by SS/SM) copied files individually, and created a bootable disk
with the proper bootstrap monitor.  Still, the same error.

Anyone's help with this would be immensely appreciated.

Thanks.




