Kurt,

Let me assume that you installed the OEM Windows 95 B or C.  If you installed
the original piece of trash Windows 95 A, consider replacing it with any one of
WIndows 95B, 95C, or 98SE.

Now, it's been a while since I peered inside one of these beasts, but I'm pretty
sure it uses the Intel 440LX chipset.  Your problem is caused by the absence of
440LX drivers in Windows 95, which was released before the 440LX was ready.
(You can verify the main motherboard chipset, not the graphics chip, by removing
the main logic board and examining it for large Intel-labled chips.  AFAIK, DEC
did not use any non-Intel chipsets for Pentium II systems.  DEC DID use Sis and
other chipsets for some lower priced Pentium boxes.)

If you install WIndows 98SE (not the First Edition!), no problem with 440LX
chipset drivers.  First Editions from Microsoft are rarely very good, and
Windows 98 First Edition is no exception.

If you install a 95 variant, you also need to download the 440LX driver
software, preferably from the Intel web site.  Go to the following URL and
download the Intel chipset driver file:

http://developer.intel.com/design/software/drivers/platform/inf.htm

Format the hard drive again, copy the downloaded file onto your hard drive
BEFORE you attempt to install WIndows 95.  Go thru the installation as you would
normally, then execute the chipset driver file.  This should get your system to
the point where all of its IDE channels are working properly.

This is typical of the everyday mismatches I encounter between operating system
and hardware, and it's never gonna get any better.  No matter what, some hot
graphics chip, sound chip, or motherboard chipset will show up between major
Windows operating system releases, and the drivers have to be loaded in after
the OS is installed.  Many name-brand manufacturers cover this sort of problem
with a specially built restore CD.  For once, I can't blame Microsoft for
anything here.  The problem is simply a matter of timing... Ben Myers

