>change recipes and the error occurred, we would just power cycle the VT103
>terminal and it would work fine.  However, on one machine in particular the
>problem happened too frequently.  I do believe the program has been written
>specifically to read recipes from DD1:.  The System will attempt to read the
>boot and system files from either drive, but such is not the case once the
>program is loaded and a recipe needs to be selected.  Would this eliminate
>the possibility of re-ASSIGNing the drive?  Since this now happens with the
>new dual floppy system, I'm beginning to suspect the program itself.  The
>other consideration I have is perhaps the serial I/O card and possibly the
>power supply.  Could it be a thermal issue?  See I still have difficulty
>with the last three things since the system has usually has no trouble
>loading the O/S.  Sometimes it stumbles during a reboot and doesn't load the
>O/S at all.  An "@" prompt and some number appears, then nothing else.
>After another try or two (power off/on), it loads.

It is probably trying to tell you that, sometimes, the power up self
test has found a problem sufficiently severe that the machine must be
halted.

"@" indicates that the LSI-11 has halted, and entered a debug mode
called "console ODT". The number is the program counter. The TU58
bootstrap just sizes and tests memory, then tries to read block 0 of
unit 0, if unit 0 cannot be read, it tries unit 1, and repeats until
one or the other reads successfully.  If the halt occurs before any
tape motion, the memory test has failed. The number is the clue, it
should be 173xxx.  I don't have the documentation to hand, but, IIRC,
3 of the of the other registers will point to the failed memory, and
its actual and intended contents. To read the registers type to
console ODT:
R0/(contents printed)line feed
R1/ etc.

Regards,

David P.


