:same machine. (I do not remember which one conflicts).

  Go back and find out the details, please.  If you are unsure of the
  specific error(s), then it is rather difficult for folks to provide
  you with specific answers or with workarounds.

:Can somebody post what he (or she) is running on the same machine?

  OpenVMS doesn't care about other operating systems co-resident on
  the bbox, and will happily coexist so long OpenVMS has at least one
  disk spindle to itself.  I expect Tru64 UNIX and Linux will also
  peacefully coexist.

  Microsoft Windows NT can overwrite the file structure of other
  operating systems with its "harmless" signature -- the signature
  is actually not harmless, and can tend to corrupt the disk of the
  other operating system.  (The OpenVMS FAQ references this detail.)

  Switching between Windows and OpenVMS or Tru64 UNIX requires you
  also switch console firmware, and -- depending on the Alpha box --
  this can be as simple as a console-level command and a power-cycle,
  or (with the half-flash systems) can require reloading the "other"
  console firmware.  (The OpenVMS FAQ references this detail, too.)

  As stated, the OpenVMS FAQ has details on switching the console and
  on the so-called half-flash Alpha systems (the AlphaServer 1000A is
  not a half-flash box), as well as on the various keyboard details
  that can be involved when interacting with the AlphaBIOS/ARC console.
  Both OpenVMS and Tru64 UNIX expect and require the SRM console.
  Microsoft Windows NT on Alpha requires the AlphaBIOS/ARC console.

