Paul Repacholi <prep@prep.synonet.com> writes:
> Don't know about booting off it, but VMS will happily page to one.

A Multia will happily boot from it.  Until the Jaz cartridge fails, which
seems to take four to six months, and has a 50% chance of breaking the
drive.

The best thing to do with a Jaz drive is to throw it away.  I've used five
of them, and they've all gone bad.  They go bad if you keep one cartridge
in them all the time.  They go bad if you swap cartridges occasionally.
They go bad if you use them a lot, or if you leave them idle much of the
time.

On the other, I've never had problems with ZIP drives.

And I've had even *worse* problems with Syquest Sparq and Syjet drives.
At ReplayTV I recommended that we use ZIP drives for development, so
that we could write a bootable system image onto the cartridge, and
then put it in the system under test.  (The original plan had been to
use normal hard drives, power down the development system, move the
drive to the target, etc., which would have slowed things down
tremendously.)  My manager liked the idea but thought we should use
Syquest Sparq drives because the higher capacity would allow us to even
put MPEG video files on the removable media, a feature that we never
actually used.  We went through *hundreds* of Sparq and Syjet drives;
they all were either DOA or failed very quickly.  Syquest had a very
good return policy, up until they went bankrupt.  Hmmm, I wonder why
that happened?

Removable rigid media is just a bad idea.  Back in the 1960s-1980s when
there was no other choice, people used it on big computers because there
was full-time staff running the machines, doing preventative maintenance,
making backups, etc.  So when a pack crashed, it usually wasn't that big
a deal.

But trying to turn that into a consumer product is a disaster.  I was
skeptical from the outset, but I assumed that they must have come up
with some way to make it reliable.  Boy, was I ever wrong.


