>    political stability and the mighty dollar will take a beating."

Yup.  When American productivity is no longer that much higher than that
elsewhere, living standards change accordingly.  The problem right now is
that our productivity *could* still be much higher, but while those
countries we're losing jobs to have people willing to hustle for their
employers our own approach is all too often more like one of entitlement.

If we instead really hustle as well, we can at least greatly slow the coming
changes to a pace that we'll find manageable - say, to one where our
standard of living remains at least close to constant while the rest of the
world starts to catch up.  That wouldn't be bad at all, compared to how
things may turn out if we don't.

