In article <ulbeu19femnqa5@corp.supernews.com>, sword7@speakeasy.org writes:

:Someone asked me about graphics adaptor for my TS10/VAX emulator.  I only 
:have a copy of VCB02 tech manual that provides graphics programming at 
:registers level.  Will VCB02 work on VAXserver 3900 machine (KA655 or CVAX 
:chip)?  I know that VCB01/VCB02 is designed for MicroVAX II series.
:If not, what graphics adaptor do that KA655 series support?

  The VCB02 (also known as the GPX and the "dragon") controller is the
  usual controller for the Q-bus series, while the VCB01 (also known
  as the QVSS) controller was used on the earlier members of the series.  

  Support for VCB01 was not available on the later of the Q-bus series
  processors, starting circa KA650, and with VAXstation 3200, VAXstation
  3500, and later systems.   The VCB01 is thus only typically found on 
  the VAXstation I series and on the monochrome variants in the VAXstation
  II series.

  AFAIK, most of the VAX 4000 series could also operate with the VCB02,
  though the faster systems required the VCB02 was up to current revision.
  Starting around the KA650 series, you had to have your VCB02 at current
  rev, lest you see various odd bus lock-ups.  (The faster processors 
  could easily push the Q-bus faster than the earlier VCB02 revisions 
  had expected.)

  The VCB03 was found only in the VAXstation 3520 and VAXstation 3540
  series.  These are the two members of the Firefox series SMP 
  multiprocessor workstations, and these use the M-bus backplane for
  the connection to the graphics controller.  The internal organization
  of these Firefox systems is quite different from that of all other
  Q-bus systems, including the similarly-named VAXstation 3500 series.

  I would target the VCB02, and not the VCB01.  I would only target 
  the VCB03 if you plan on providing a fully-capable SMP emulator.

