Bill Gunshannon wrote:

> In light of this discussion, was there ever any support in the QBUS PDP-11's
> for any of this graphic hardware??  Did any of the PDP OSes ever support
> any kind of graphics beyond things like Tek-4010's??

It was certainly within the realm of "possibility" for the
PDP-11 to have supported at least the VCB01 and
probably the VCB02. Brian (S.) McCarthy and I
actually had a couple of conversations along those
lines.

But by that time, corporate thought was "all things
VAX" and "To heck with PDP-11s!" so nothing ever
happened.

As folks have already mentioned, there were several
earlier graphics adapters. Besides the Pro graphics,
and the VT11 (as seen in the GT40), there was also
the much-more capable and yet upwards-compatible
VS11 vector graphics system. We used it extensively
on our "SUDS" graphics design stations (and they, in turn
were used extensively to run "Lunar Lander". They
didn't typically have switch registers so "Space War"
wasn't possible.). I think there was also a CSS bit-mapped
graphics display.

Even the ancient PDP-8 had an Omnibus bit-mapped
grahics adapter. VT8E comes to mind, but I'm not
sure about that. There was also a fancy PDP-8
vector-graphics system from CSS that used a
Tektronix 611 DVBST (Direct-View Bistable
Storage Tube). I spent *A LOT* of time tweaking
calibration pots on those puppies and playing with
the 611's. As you might guess from the display
employed, they operated a lot like the Tek 40xx
Mean Green Flashin' Machines, but driven from
the bus rather than a serial line.

And then there were the PDP-15 and PDP-12 graphic
subsystems...

And the terminals-derived devices such as the VT55,
VT105, VT125, and what was the name of the original
little ReGIS+BASIC box?

Atlant




