HP's pulling of Compaq call-center contract puzzles Broward

By Doreen Hemlock 
Business Writer 
Posted August 9 2002 

  
 
News that computer giant Hewlett-Packard Co. is pulling a contract
from a Fort Lauderdale call center and shifting some of its business
to lower-cost India prompted a mix of reactions Thursday.

Economic development officials gave assurances that Florida's strong
call-center business continues to grow, while a Broward County
commissioner suggested a boycott of HP products.

Commissioner Sue Gunzburger said she'll ask the County Commission on
Tuesday to halt new purchases of HP printers for Broward, now that HP
is ending a contract with The Answer Group that could mean the loss of
up to 1,200 Broward jobs within 90 days.

"If they want to take jobs offshore, they should sell their printers
offshore -- not to Broward County," she said.

"I would hope that others would feel the same way I do and consider
boycotting the company."

Gunzburger said in her 10 years as commissioner, she knows of no other
case when a local firm has lost so many jobs to another nation.

South Florida economic development leaders said they also were
surprised: They knew of no other big loss of call-center business
offshore, and indeed, have seen the call-center industry expand -- not
shrink -- even amid the current economic slump.

"We don't see this as a trend," said high-tech consultant Michael
Langley, former president of the Broward Alliance and now an adviser
to the state's development group, Enterprise Florida. "We're not
immune from companies losing contracts. And this is a good alert to
make sure we monitor closely. But we haven't seen this as a retreating
industry, but one that is growing."

Broward County had an estimated 20,000 call center jobs last year,
according to the latest research from The Broward Alliance. And new
call-centers have been setting up since then, including some in the
travel industry, such as an $11 million Delta Air Lines center slated
to employ more than 600 in Miramar starting next year.

"We've been a favored location for call-centers," said the Alliance's
acting president Joan Goodrich, adding she's just received an inquiry
from a company in the northeast U.S. interested in a Broward center.

Miami-Dade has about 15,000 people working at call centers, including
nearly 6,000 employed by Fort Lauderdale-based Precision Response
Corp. The number is rising, said Frank Nero, president of The Beacon
Council, the county's development group.

"I haven't seen any trend of this business going offshore," said Nero,
noting companies are attracted to South Florida's multi-lingual work
force and relatively low labor, rental and tax rates compared with
other competing U.S. locales.

Doreen Hemlock can be reached at dhemlock@sun-sentinel.com or
305-810-5009.
