"Ben Myers" <ben_myers@charter.net> wrote in message
news:3d55f246.43171393@news.charter.net...

...

> The second real issue is that in this push into a global economy, we
Americans
> are getting downsized,  outsourced,  H1-B'ed, and Enroned out of jobs, and
> nobody but nobody at the national or local level is considering what to do
to
> replace those jobs.

1.  Saying that 'nobody, but nobody' in government is paying any attention
to this situation is a ridiculous (and incorrect) generalization:  efforts
exist at all levels.

2.  Are they effective?  Should more be being done?  Those are at least
legitimate questions.  However, for anyone save possibly the overtly
handicapped, getting a job is really a personal responsibility, not a
governmental one.

Down-sizing is often really stupid (sacrificing long-term productivity for
this-quarter returns), but corporations have the right to be stupid if they
choose (though usually eventually pay the price - and in some ways we all
do).  Out-sourcing can be stupid as well (or in some cases both can be
exactly the right thing to do).  The only reason H1-Bs are an issue in the
first place is because we erect barriers to foreign labor that inhibit free
competition in the labor market, so you'll get no sympathy from me there.
And last I knew if a corporation failed for whatever reason (Enron-style
corruption or otherwise) that was considered just tough luck for its
workers, not a matter for government intervention (save to prosecute any
wrong-doing).

  The H1-B program is somewhat of a farcical indentured
> servitude for may of the people who come here to work, with
well-below-market
> wages and no way to leave the employ of the corporate plantation owner.

That aspect of the program I strongly object to.  But the solution I favor
is simply to open up employment to anyone living and paying taxes here,
citizen or not (and allow anyone who can hold a job that will support them
to be a foreign resident).

...

> Pure capitalism is a nice pure concept.  Some people considered communism
to be
> a great pure concept with everyone working together and sharing together.
In
> either case, greed and lust for power seem to make the reality less
successful
> than the pure abstraction.  Like Ayn Rand's Atlas Shrugged makes a great
read,
> but when did you ever meet an idealistic capitalist like the ones she
portrays
> in her book?  Haven't met one lately, and I won't meet one in my lifetime,
> unless you want to count Aaron Feuerstein of Malden Mills

A remarkable man, but one with the luxury (because he personally owned the
company rather than being beholden to a BoD or stockholders) of being able
to take an unconventional view of what 'doing the right thing' meant.  Ken
Olsen might be a good candidate as well (and look what the BoD did to him).

No one is talking about 'pure capitalism' here, just about where the curbs
that government in many cases rightly applies to moderate capitalism's
excesses should stop.  Protecting U.S. jobs is not something the government
can do in any real sense, because the economy *is* global in nature and if
labor is less expensive elsewhere then either U.S. companies must take
advantage of it or lose their markets to external competitors who can.
Retaliating by raising tariffs on those foreign products isn't a solution,
because it hurts the U.S. consumer (who has just as much 'right' to be
considered by government as the U.S. worker).  This is basic economics:
relatively free markets are the great leveler, and in the current world
create a rising tide that lifts most boats except, sometimes, those which
were riding the highest in the first place (which, unfortunately, is us, but
there's not much we can do about it without causing even worse problems for
ourselves).

If anyone saves us, it will have to be ourselves:  the rest of the world is
busy getting on with getting ahead, and have little sympathy with spoiled
rich kids who have suddenly discovered that the butler isn't going to be
bringing up their dinner any more.  Fortunately for us, we still have
*incredible* residual advantages in wealth, education, and infrastructure
compared with most of the rest of the world:  all we need to do is put them
to use rather than just sit on them and bitch.

